# Linux File Permissions

Linux permissions control who can read, write, and execute files and directories.

## Permission Types

r = Read = 4
w = Write = 2
x = Execute = 1

## Permission Groups

u = User/Owner
g = Group
o = Others
a = All

## Check Permissions

ls -l
ls -la
stat file.txt

Example:
-rwxr-xr--

Owner  = rwx
Group  = r-x
Others = r--

## chmod - Change Permissions

chmod +x file.sh
chmod -x file.sh
chmod u+r file.txt
chmod u+w file.txt
chmod u+x file.sh
chmod u-r file.txt
chmod g+r file.txt
chmod g+w file.txt
chmod g+x file.sh
chmod g-r file.txt
chmod o+r file.txt
chmod o+w file.txt
chmod o+x file.sh
chmod o-r file.txt
chmod o-w file.txt
chmod o-x file.sh
chmod a+r file.txt
chmod a+w file.txt
chmod a+x file.sh

## Numeric Permissions

4 = r--
2 = -w-
1 = --x
6 = rw-
5 = r-x
3 = -wx
7 = rwx
0 = ---

## Common chmod Commands

chmod 777 file
# Owner, group and others: rwx

chmod 755 file
# Owner: rwx | Group: r-x | Others: r-x

chmod 644 file
# Owner: rw- | Group: r-- | Others: r--

chmod 700 file
# Owner: rwx | Group: --- | Others: ---

chmod 600 file
# Owner: rw- | Group: --- | Others: ---

chmod 400 file
# Owner: r-- | Group: --- | Others: ---

chmod 444 file
# Owner, group and others: r--

## Recursive Permissions

chmod -R 755 folder/
chmod -R 644 folder/

# -R applies permission changes to files and subdirectories.

## chown - Change Owner

chown user file.txt
sudo chown user file.txt
sudo chown user:group file.txt
sudo chown -R user:group folder/

## chgrp - Change Group

chgrp group file.txt
sudo chgrp group file.txt
sudo chgrp -R group folder/

## Check Owner and Group

ls -l
stat file.txt

## Special Permissions

chmod u+s file
chmod g+s folder
chmod +t folder

SUID  → chmod 4755 file
SGID  → chmod 2755 file
Sticky Bit → chmod 1777 folder

## Default Permissions

umask
umask -S
umask 022

## Useful Examples

touch test.txt
ls -l test.txt

chmod 644 test.txt
ls -l test.txt

chmod +x script.sh
./script.sh

mkdir test
chmod 755 test

sudo chown $USER:$USER test.txt

## Permission Structure

-rwxr-xr--

- = File type
rwx = Owner permissions
r-x = Group permissions
r-- = Others permissions

## File Type Symbols

- = Regular file
d = Directory
l = Symbolic link
c = Character device
b = Block device

## Important Commands

ls -l
ls -la
chmod
chown
chgrp
stat
umask

## Key Point

Linux permissions follow:

Owner → Group → Others

Permissions are:

Read → Write → Execute

Numeric values:

Read = 4
Write = 2
Execute = 1
